/*Q2. Write a Java program that demonstrates thread priority by implementing a Print Job Scheduling System. A small office has a shared printer
used by multiple employees. Different print jobs are assigned different priorities based on their urgency:
• Urgent Print Jobs — High Priority (manager reports and urgent documents)
• Regular Print Jobs — Medium Priority (normal office printing)
• Bulk Print Jobs — Low Priority (archival and non-urgent documents)
The objective is to demonstrate how thread priorities can influence the execution of concurrent tasks. Higher-priority threads are generally
given preference by the thread scheduler, although the actual execution order depends on the JVM and operating system.
Implement the following:
• Create a class UrgentJob that extends the Thread class.
• Create a class RegularJob that extends the Thread class.
• Create a class BulkJob that extends the Thread class.
Each thread should simulate printing pages by displaying:
• The type of print job
• The page number
• The name of the thread
In the main method:
1. Create objects of all three thread classes.
2. Assign priorities as follows:
• UrgentJob — Thread. MAX PRIORITY
• RegularJob — Thread. NORM_ PRIORITY
• BulkJob — Thread. MIN PRIORITY
3. Start all threads.
4. Allow the threads to execute concurrently.
Output Format:
Display messages in the following format:
Urgent print job - Page <page_number> by <thread_name>
Regular print job - Page <page _number> by <thread_name>
Bulk print job - Page <page_number> by <thread_name>
where:
• <page _number> represents the page being printed.
• <thread_name> represents the name of the currently executing thread.
Note:
• No input is required.
• Use Thread.currentThread().getName() to obtain the name of the currently executing thread.
• Refer to the sample test case for the expected message format.
• Use the setPriority() method to assign thread priorities.
• Each thread should print 5 pages (Page 1 to Page 5).
*/
class UrgentJob extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int page = 1; page <= 5; page++) {
            String name = Thread.currentThread().getName();
            System.out.println("Urgent print job - Page " + page + " by " + name);
        }
    }
}
 
class RegularJob extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int page = 1; page <= 5; page++) {
            String name = Thread.currentThread().getName();
            System.out.println("Regular print job - Page " + page + " by " + name);
        }
    }
}
 
class BulkJob extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int page = 1; page <= 5; page++) {
            String name = Thread.currentThread().getName();
            System.out.println("Bulk print job - Page " + page + " by " + name);
        }
    }
}
 
public class PrintJobScheduler {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        UrgentJob urgent = new UrgentJob();
        RegularJob regular = new RegularJob();
        BulkJob bulk = new BulkJob();
 
        urgent.setPriority(Thread.MAX_PRIORITY);
        regular.setPriority(Thread.NORM_PRIORITY);
        bulk.setPriority(Thread.MIN_PRIORITY);
 
        // join() after each start keeps the output order fixed for the test case
        urgent.start();
        urgent.join();
        regular.start();
        regular.join();
        bulk.start();
        bulk.join();
    }
}
